ving a political scandal especially  in election year in 1944 where people  are gonna say this is going on in Europe  and not only have you done very little  to stop it directly but people in your  state department or even aiding those  who are stopping the flow of refugees in  my line of work we always look for  figures in history who take a risk and  these days I think oftentimes we feel  that we find them too seldom because you  know in the year 2002 everyone has his  political or her political consultants  and polls everyone is told not to do the  stupid thing don't do something that's  gonna jeopardize your job but when you  find something like Henry Morgenthau  being willing to jeopardize what was it  the absolute center of his life which  was this cherished relationship almost  an older brother like relationship that  FDR had with him he threw the dice and  he confronted the president and it had  an effect because within weeks there was  a war refugee board to begin  trying to bring Jews out of Germany in a  big way and also Roosevelt finally gave  in March of 1944 the first date maybe he  ever gave saying that the Nazis are  involved in the worst crime in human  history the systematic murder of the  Jews another thing that came up at about  the same time was there were a number of  people who were looking for other ways  to stop the Holocaust and one suggestion  was that you bomb outfits you bomb some  of the other death camps perhaps also  the rail lines with the idea that you'd  probably end up killing the people who  were there but at least you'd stop the  means by which they are being killed and  given what we knew from history before I  did the work on this book the feeling  was that that suggestion was made a lot  to people in the White House and it  never got up to the president it went to  as high as an Assistant Secretary of War  named John John McCloy who turned it  down flat who said you know we are  trying to fight a war and I'm not going  to use the Pentagon in any way that is  not deliberately for the winning of the  war what I discovered and this is one of  the wonderful things about writing  history you sometimes find a document  that's just we call them Eureka  documents one document that stands  everything that we knew on its ear turns  out that about three years before mccloy  died he gave an interview to someone in  which he finally fessed up he said well  it turns out actually this decision went  beyond me I actually did go to see FDR  to ask him whether he wanted to bomb a  schvitz and the president turned out and  down the idea of flat said I think it'll  just kill a lot of people and it's not  going to do very much good I don't want  any part of it he said and this shows us  something that's good and something  that's bad and this is what history is  like the good thing is I think one of  the biggest decisions in history to face  a president was do you bomb the death  camps in 1944 and was there a chance to  stop the Holocaust  I always felt dreadful that this  important decision never got beyond and  a secretary  an Assistant Secretary of War so thank  God it did according to McCoy's  interview at the end of his life  the downside is that if this was as I  believe one of the great decisions to  face a president historically it's the  kind of thing you would have wanted a  president like FDR to give to experts  and argue will this help will this hurt  one of the ramifications and the thing  about FDR pardon was wonderful part of  him was dreadful the wonderful part was  he was so brilliant at using people and  keeping a hundred balls in the air at  any one time almost running World War  two cies single-handedly the downside  was that faced with a decision like this  he thought he could make a decision like  this too without the proper information  so rather than convening a committee and  examining all the possibilities this was  cavalier he came in do we bomb or not  Roosevelt said no quite casually turned  it down flat and that was the end of  that you could argue it round or flat in  retrospect I think there's a very great  possibility of that had the death camps  been bombed in 1944 many many lives  could be saved and many more perhaps it  might have been lost in the bombing so  from there what the story does is this  Morgenthau by then radicalized very  worried about what the Germans were  doing a ruled war - and worried even  more about what they might do after  World War two he went on a trip to  Europe to take a look at the  battlefields wanted to take a look at  France now that France was being  liberated after d-day in August of 1944  and while then he discovered that the  State Department other members of the  Roosevelt administration were making  plans what we do with Germany after VE  Day and the plans were this their idea  was Germany is going to be another  occupation problem just like what the  Americans did in Mexico or perhaps Spain  and the idea is to get Germany on its  feet it's quite as quickly as possible  so the Germans can feed themselves and  begin trading so that you know by 1947  or so Americans can pull out of Europe  and everything will be wonderful  Morgenthau who had been horrified by the  Holocaust and was very worried about  what the Germans might do even after  World  or to as many Americans were went to  Roosevelt and said you know you want  unconditional surrender you want to make  a democracy here from the ground up why  are you supporting these plans and  Morgenthau reacted and the way he  reacted was he had his people draw up an  enormous plan which once again you can  find both of the National Archives and  the Roosevelt Library which soon was  called the morgenthau plan' and what the  morgenthau plan' was essentially when we  defeat the Germans after VE Day  destroyed German industry flood the coal  mines reduced Germany to the level of a  basic agricultural estate that can't  make war let the Germans do in their own  juice suffer so that they will always  remember that they lost this war they'll  never want to make war again and the  interesting thing is that you would have  thought that Roosevelt on reading this  would have said it's too vindictive it  might create another war because might  make the Germans very resentful as they  were after World War one the interesting  thing is that when Morgenthau gave this  to Roosevelt fall of 1944 Roosevelt said  I think this is a good idea let's go  ahead with it  Roosevelt met with Winston Churchill at  Quebec shoda the morgenthau plan'  Churchill said I can't believe what I'm  looking at I mean not only is this  inhuman and undemocratic but you  decimate Germany this way then after VE  Day the Red Army the Soviet Union  they're going to roll right through  Europe you're gonna have a communist  Europe because there will be no Germany  to stop it nonetheless Roosevelt had  Churchill sign a document endorsing this  plan and what happened there was what  happened so often during the war rosid  all had more power than Churchill did  Churchill was desperately dependent on  the Americans and American aid and  Roosevelt essentially said unless you  sign on the dotted line I'm gonna  withdraw all aid to England after World  War two  and Churchill said you know I see good  things about the morgenthau plan' I  hadn't seen before  he said I'm converted and not only was  he converted but he actually drafted the  document himself saying how great the  morgenthau plan' was and so that's what  went on in Quebec  back here in Washington were Cordell  Hull Secretary of State at Henry Stimson  the Secretary of War who had been the  ones saying you know we've got to get  Germany on its feet as quickly as  possible they were told that Roosevelt  had put a gun at Churchill's had got him  to support this morgenthau plan' and  they were outraged and how did they  reply in 2002 you know when someone in a  president's cabinet does not like what  is done by the President or another  cabinet member occasionally it doesn't  happen very often but once in a blue  moon occasionally someone will leak the  idea to a newspaper so it will be  published and so when everyone will see  what a terrible idea this is and shoot  it down well the news is that's exactly  what was done in 1944 as well  Holland Stimpson leak this to the press  it appeared very quickly and the public  immediately knew what Roosevelt and  Churchill had done secretly at Quebec  and the result was that there was an  ugly argument between hull and Stimpson  on the one side and Morgenthau on the  other these were different times but as  you look at their Diaries it's not a  lovely picture Stimson would write in  his diary Jews shouldn't push the way  that Morgenthau is especially on an  issue like Germany he said it's Semitism  wild for vengeance he said and he went  to Morgenthau and he said the problem is  you just don't understand what we should  do with Germany he said the way to deal  with Germany is with Christianity and  kindness which is about the worst thing  he could have said to Morgan foe who was  deeply insulted and then once this all  became public the Germans got into the  act  Joseph Goebbels made a speech saying  that this morgenthau plan' is what the  Allies planned for Germany after the war  you Germans should fight hard to make  sure that 43 million of you are not  murdered and he said this is a Jewish  murder plan that shows the kind of thing  that FDR wants to do deeply ugly and it  happened largely in public during the  war even during a campaign in the fall  of 1944 and the interesting thing is  that as I studied Roosevelt hour-by-hour  by the fall of nineteen  forty-four Roosevelt was a much sicker  man than I had realized his medical  records once again in the National  Archives in the FDR library were  released not long ago and as doctors by  1944 found that Roosevelt was in what  they called cardiac failure they felt  that he only was bound to live a matter  of time and the people around Roosevelt  they didn't let it be known by the  American public at the time especially  because people were voting on him but  you'd see these scenes that I got in my  book where Roosevelt the blood was  beginning to get less and less coming to  his brain and you'd see these scenes  were Roosevelt would sign an important  document what do we do about Germany or  what do we do about the soldiers  fighting in Europe and then a day later  someone would come to him and say how  could you have signed that it's the  opposite of what you're thinking and  Roosevelt would look at the document  with his signature and say I never  signed this thing in my life I've never  seen this more and more often this  happened it was not very public he might  have lost the election 44 if people knew  what you see at the end of 44 more and  more is not only Roosevelt who is less  and less able to cope in this way but  also a Roosevelt who begins and again  you see this in Diaries that have just  been opened quite recently a Roosevelt  who in private knew what was happening  to him and was beginning to think that  perhaps he should not remain as  president he says to a number of his  intimates a number of times late 44 and  early 1945  I'll get reelected I'll become president  for a fourth term but I don't want to  stay because I just don't have it  anymore he said perhaps I'll quit the  presidency in 44 or 45 after World War  two ends and interesting the one thing  he talked about doing after the war and  after leaving the presidency leaving it  to Harry Truman was becoming Secretary  General the first Secretary General of  the United Nations and so much did he  think about this he actually talked  about putting the UN at Hyde Park near  his house and so it'd be convenient to  him and that be a little airstrip  foreign leaders could come and fly in  and see him and that's the way he saw  his retirement of course that didn't  happen because his illness was much more  advanced and he realized and he died  abruptly  in April 1945 and did so I've got a  scene in the book the day after his  final dinner which he spent with Henry  Morgenthau they ate dinner and argued  the whole time about what you'd do about  Germany after World War two  April 12 1945 we were about three weeks  away from VE Day and even then Roosevelt  had not yet decided so Roosevelt is dead  and into the vacuum comes Harry Truman a  president who had had very little  training and foreign policy Roosevelt  had barely spoken to him they had been  together in Washington maybe about three  days of Truman's vice presidency  Truman hadn't been told about the a-bomb  he knew virtually nothing about what  Roosevelt's plans were for Germany or  Europe or very much else and so you have  these scenes of Truman in the basement  of the White House reading his eyes out  millions of pages of documents to try to  find the secret of what Roosevelt wanted  and the fact is of course it was in no  document it was really in Roosevelt's  head and so Truman had to very quickly  improvise what Truman knew he wanted was  not Morgenthau or Morgenthau his plan  and so often in history you find that  great historical events turn on petty  animosities I know that doesn't happen  in Washington these days but during  World War two once in awhile it did and  the key to Truman's attitude toward  Morgenthau was as follows Truman was  elected senator from Missouri 1934 as  many of you know as an icon of the Tom  Pendergast machine of Kansas City many  people thought that he was sort of a  corrupt figure that had been sent here  by this party boss Democratic boss in  Kansas City Truman was super-sensitive  every time someone was not as friendly  to him as he thought that he should be  treated he thought they're treating me  badly because they think that I'm sort  of below the salt  I'm the slightly sleazy figure that they  don't want to have any anything to do  with and what happened was Truman went  to call on the Treasury secretary in  1935 Henry Morgenthau  and he was told that Morgenthau was not  in  and from that day onward he assumed that  Morgenthau was in but assumed that  Truman was such a dirty figure but  Morgenthau did not want to shake hands  with him Morgenthau had no idea of this  but when Truman became president it was  not great news for Morgenthau because  you had a new president who had been  disposed against him from that day in  1935 and so very quickly what happened  was Truman decided to turn against  Morgenthau and his plan he fired  Morgenthau and essentially decided with  Stimson went to Potsdam and carried out  a plan to make Germany a democracy at  the same time you know you're always  trying to get into these people's heads  Truman made a lot of decisions that were  the decisions of what I think is a very  great president especially that given  the fact that this was someone who was  so badly prepared at the same time you  find these things about people that show  how complex they were for instance  Truman in April 1945 just a few days  after he was president you look at the  newspapers and on the front page of the  newspapers are Buchenwald and Belson the  death camps are opening for the first  time Americans actually saw pictures of  the inmates being released and if you go  into the records of that time even many  American anti-semites when they saw  these pictures they would say how could  I have said such things or thought such  things look at how this people has  suffered I mean it changed a lot of  American opinion but you look at Harry  Truman's Diaries and this is something I  just cannot explain even though he was  seeing the same pictures you'll see  things in his diaries where he's saying  things like the Jews think they're God's  chosen people I think God had better  judgment and other ethnic epithets he  assures Henry Stimson that neither  Morgenthau nor any of those Jew boys  will go to Potsdam to meet with with  Churchill and Stalin and it's horrible  but it's also what makes these guys so  interesting because this is the same  president that later on went on to  recognize the State of Israel in 1948  and you wonder how someone could have  during the day made decisions that were  so humane and sometimes at night written  things into his  diary that were almost like dr. Jekyll  and mr. Hyde  so the dennou mall was this you went  through this war we won the war in 1945  by then Germany was not the danger that  it had been before because as Roosevelt  had no reason to expect during the war  in 1945 Harry Truman had something that  was a permanent check against a German  Germany that tried to make trouble and  that was the atomic bomb by 1945 in  Potsdam the great worry was no longer  Germany with the Soviet Union and so  what we Americans did in post-war  Germany was to use that blank slate that  Franklin Roosevelt and offered us  unconditional surrender to occupy  Germany and try to create a democracy  and one quote I've got in the book that  says that I think just perfectly Dwight  Eisenhower who was the first military  governor of Germany after we Allies  invaded and occupied liberated he was  talking to his staff October of 1945 in  Frankfurt and he said you know you think  you guys think that World War two has  been successful we don't know that yet  he said the test of whether you're world  war two in Europe was successful it's  not today it's not ten years from now  it's going to be fifty years from now he  said if in 1995 Germany is a peaceful  democracy then we will have succeeded  and you advance the clock fifty years he  got it almost right on the nose Germany  was unified in 1990 it's a country with  an awful lot of shortcomings but we have  our soldiers we have Franklin Roosevelt  we have Harry Truman to thank for coming  up with that strategy through all sorts  of twists and turns and shortcomings  that I write about in the book but we  have them more than any other American  to thank for the fact that we in this  day and age not only have a Germany  that's a democracy but a Europe that's  turning into that direction as well and  I close the book with a scene I make the  point that leaders who are fighting wars  oftentimes when they get discouraged  they think about what would happen if  you lose the war and this was true of  FDR and  and even though these were pretty  self-confident people Roosevelt for  instance when he met with Churchill at  Quebec in 1944 they had a chat about  what Hitler might have done had he  invaded London and succeeded in 1940 and  Harry Truman went in 1945 he went  through the ruins of defeated Berlin he  began to think what would Hitler have  done to Washington and to the United  States if Hitler had won World War two  and it's a game it's a fascinating game  for a Nestorian to play and I write  right about at the end of the book you  know what would America be like if  Hitler had won I mean it would be a land  of utterly unspeakable cruelty needless  to say you can imagine what Washington  would look like the capital of the White  House the Washington Monument probably  would have swastikas it certainly would  be bereft to synagogues or Jews probably  the Nazis would have had Roosevelt's  house at Hyde Park and Truman's house at  Independence Missouri demolished to make  sure that these would not be rallying  points for people who would oppose Nazi  domination of America it's a fascinating  game to play a horrible game what would  this country have been like if things  had gone the other way and another way  to think about it as I write in the book  is what were Hitler's plans if he had  won the war what would Germany have  looked like and we actually know because  some of his people wrote about this and  in some of the documents that once again  are here in the National Archives some  of the captured German documents and we  know that had Hitler won the war he  planned to remake Berlin basically level  it and remake it into a city called  Germania with enormous halls that were  probably a hundred times the size of the  American capital buildings that look  sort of like the cessful be to be a  pageant of an oriental despot for  instance there was a long hallway so  that if you were a chief of state come  to call on Hitler  you'd have to hike for about a half a  mile so that you'd be out of breath by  the time you were admitted to the  Furious presence it would have been  horrible and absolute but now because  what Germania Berlin would have looked  like would have been sort of a bad  version of  a architectural student working out of a  textbook but the wonderful thing is you  begin to think how Germany and how  Berlin today looks so much different  from what Hitler intended and it really  makes the point of how much we were able  to do when we won this war and any of  you who have been to Berlin recently you  look at that architecture it's of all  kinds there are kids walking around with  purple spiked hair and rings through  their cheeks is exactly what Hitler  would have absolutely detested and  Hitler wanted World War two memorials in  Berlin and there are but the wonderful  thing is thanks to the fact that we won  the war the more memorials are not to  Himmler and they're not to Goebbels and  they're not to Hitler but the memorials  that are there are to the people who  actually tried to kill Hitler and tried  to build a democracy and to bring this  all full circle back to what I was  talking about the beginning if you want  to find for instance Hitler's bunker  where he died you can find where it is  but first you're gonna have to go  through this wonderful corporate  architecture Sony and all the buildings  that never would have been there had  Hitler won and you'll also have to go  past a very big columns memorial to the  victims of the Holocaust that's just  rising this year and the other thing is  that if you want to go to Hitler's  military headquarters there it is the  headquarters where Stauffenberg was  murdered on that night of the day he  tried to kill Hitler in July of 1944 but  the wonderful thing is that it's no  longer military headquarters it's now  been turned into an museum to honor the  conspirators who tried to kill Hitler  and it was on mendler Street the street  is not any longer called bend ler the  street nowadays is called Stauffenberg  Street thank you very much  Michael Beschloss is author of the  conquerors Roosevelt Truman and the  destruction of Hitler's Germany 1941 to  1945 it's published by Simon &amp; Schuster
 

